From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Windows.Web.Http: response headers, so winget can read a source

After a request, winget reads the response's headers -- their status is not
enough; it iterates them (IIterable<IKeyValuePair<HSTRING,HSTRING>>) and looks
them up. Patch 0028 returned E_NOTIMPL for HttpResponseMessage.Headers, so
winget could not open a source.

This adds the response-header collection (http_response_headers.c), built from
winhttp's raw header block: its default interface is the typed
IHttpResponseHeaderCollection, with IMap<HSTRING,HSTRING> for lookup and
IIterable/IIterator/IKeyValuePair for iteration. The filter captures the raw
headers (WINHTTP_QUERY_RAW_HEADERS_CRLF) and hands them to the response.

With this, winget reads the status and every header of its package source over
real HTTPS. What remains before results appear is the source's data package
(an MSIX the CLI downloads and opens) and its string resources.
